/**
 * @module jwt
 *
 * JSON Web Tokens (JWT), as defined in RFC 7519. The module signs,
 * verifies and inspects tokens, using the HMAC, RSA-PSS, ECDSA and
 * Ed25519 families of algorithms.
 *
 * Supported algorithms:
 *
 * - HS256, HS384 and HS512: HMAC with SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512
 * - PS256, PS384 and PS512: RSASSA-PSS with SHA-256, SHA-384 and
 *   SHA-512
 * - ES256: ECDSA with P-256 and SHA-256
 * - ES384: ECDSA with P-384 and SHA-384
 * - EdDSA: Ed25519 (RFC 8037)
 * - none: an unsecured token, with no signature, which has to be
 *   enabled explicitly
 *
 * The module supports the PS algorithms, not the RS algorithms
 * (RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5), on purpose. PKCS#1 v1.5 is an older,
 * deterministic scheme without a formal security proof, and its rigid
 * structure has led to implementation flaws in the past, such as
 * Bleichenbacher's attack on PKCS#1 v1.5 encryption. RSASSA-PSS is
 * probabilistic, has a security proof, and is harder to misuse.
 *
 * HMAC signatures are compared in constant time, to prevent timing
 * attacks on the shared secret, and every asymmetric algorithm uses the
 * verification routine of the underlying public-key implementation.
 *
 * ### A first example
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import jwt
 *
 * # sign a token
 * var token = jwt.sign({ user_id: 42, role: 'admin' }, 'secret')
 *
 * # verify and decode the token
 * var payload = jwt.verify(token, 'secret')
 * echo payload.user_id   # 42
 * ```
 *
 * If an application verifies many tokens with the same settings, use a
 * [[jwt.Verifier]] instead of passing the same options to every
 * `verify()` call. In the same way, use a [[jwt.Signer]] to sign tokens
 * repeatedly.
 *
 * When keys are looked up by `kid` in a JSON Web Key Set, instead of
 * being fixed in advance, see [[jwt.Jwks]] and
 * [[jwt.verify_with_jwks]].
 *
 * @copyright 2026, Richard Ore and The Zuri Contributors
 */

import @.errors { * }
import @.codec { * }
import @.token { * }
import @.core { * }
import @.verifier { * }
import @.signer { * }
import @.jwks { * }
